// [OPUS-5] sq-rsd3v.6 (#3265): owl:sameAs in-circuit EQUALITY reasoning --
// union-find canonicalisation + representative-membership. SEPARATELY gated
// from the RDFS/OWL-RL-minus-sameAs path (sq-rsd3v.2 / derivation.nr) on
// purpose. Epic sq-rsd3v; parent #2616.
//! In-circuit `owl:sameAs` canonicalisation -- the SEPARATELY-GATED equality
//! member of `research/zk-inference-and-credentials.md` sec 3.5.
//!
//! # Why this is NOT a rule in `derivation.nr` (the whole point of the bead)
//! `derivation.nr` re-checks every rule's variable-sharing constraints by
//! **encoding equality**: two term encodings are equal iff the two terms are
//! IDENTICAL. That proxy is exact for RDFS and for the OWL-RL rules whose
//! antecedents are fixed-shape Datalog over ids -- and it BREAKS under equality
//! reasoning, because `owl:sameAs` quotients the term universe: under OWL-RL's
//! `eq-*` rules two SYNTACTICALLY DISTINCT terms may denote the same thing, so
//! term identity is strictly FINER than the entailment relation. Bolting
//! `eq-rep-{s,p,o}` onto the fixed-shape path would therefore either (a) be
//! useless (encoding equality never fires across a `sameAs` link), or (b), if
//! the equality test were relaxed ad hoc to "equal up to some claimed
//! `sameAs`", be UNSOUND -- the claimed equalities would be trusted rather than
//! proved. This module takes the third road: prove a CANONICAL REPRESENTATIVE
//! map in-circuit, then run the ordinary encoding-equality test over
//! representatives. That is the "cost cliff" the design record names, and it is
//! why this member is gated on its own bead rather than riding sq-rsd3v.2.
//!
//! # The relation, in one line
//! The prover witnesses a spanning FOREST of the committed `owl:sameAs` edge
//! graph -- a `(mem, rep, parent)` table -- and the circuit checks that every
//! merge in it is justified by a `sameAs` triple that is actually IN the
//! committed graph. `canon_of` then reads the representative off that table.
//!
//! # The soundness argument (design sec 3.5: "encoding-equality is preserved
//! # under the canonical representative")
//! Write `~` for the equivalence relation on terms generated by
//! `{(a, b) : (a, owl:sameAs, b) is a triple of some committed graph}` --
//! i.e. the reflexive-symmetric-transitive closure, which is exactly what
//! OWL-RL's `eq-ref` / `eq-sym` / `eq-trans` derive.
//!
//! * **L1 (no over-merge).** For every ACTIVE table entry `u`,
//!   `mem[u] ~ rep[u]`. By induction on `u`, well-founded because a non-root's
//!   `parent[u] < u` STRICTLY (constraint 3a). Root (`parent[u] == u`):
//!   `rep[u] == mem[u]`, so reflexivity. Non-root with `p = parent[u]`:
//!   constraint 3c witnesses `(mem[u], sameAs, mem[p])` OR
//!   `(mem[p], sameAs, mem[u])` in a committed graph, so `mem[u] ~ mem[p]`
//!   (eq-sym covers the second orientation); the IH gives `mem[p] ~ rep[p]`;
//!   constraint 3b gives `rep[u] == rep[p]`; transitivity closes it.
//! * **L2 (`canon_of` is class-preserving).** For every term `t`,
//!   `canon_of(t) ~ t`. If no active entry has `mem[u] == t` then
//!   `canon_of(t) == t` (reflexivity); otherwise constraint 4 (uniqueness)
//!   makes that entry unique and L1 applies.
//! * **L3 (idempotence -- `canon_of` really is a canonicalisation).**
//!   `canon_of(canon_of(t)) == canon_of(t)`. `canon_of(t)` is either `t` or
//!   `mem[r]` for the root `r` of `t`'s chain; `r` is itself an active entry
//!   with `rep[r] == mem[r]`, and by uniqueness it is THE entry for that term.
//!   (This is the lemma constraint 4 exists for; L1/L2 do not need it.)
//! * **THEOREM (the bead's deliverable).** `canon_of(x) == canon_of(y)`
//!   implies `x ~ y`. Proof: `x ~ canon_of(x) == canon_of(y) ~ y` by L2 plus
//!   symmetry and transitivity of `~`. So encoding equality OVER CANONICAL
//!   REPRESENTATIVES is a sound test for "the same thing under equality
//!   reasoning" -- which is precisely the property the raw encoding-equality
//!   test lacks, and precisely what the fixed-shape rules of `derivation.nr`
//!   need if they are ever to be lifted over `~`.
//! * **COROLLARY (representative-membership -- what `sameas_entails_row`
//!   proves).** If some committed triple `(s0, p0, o0)` satisfies
//!   `canon(s0) == canon(s)`, `canon(p0) == canon(p)`, `canon(o0) == canon(o)`,
//!   then `(s, p, o)` is entailed by the committed graph under OWL-RL equality
//!   reasoning: by the THEOREM `s0 ~ s`, `p0 ~ p`, `o0 ~ o`, and the three
//!   replacement rules `eq-rep-s` / `eq-rep-p` / `eq-rep-o` applied in turn
//!   rewrite `(s0, p0, o0)` into `(s, p, o)`. Every `sameAs` fact those rules
//!   consume is itself a committed triple (constraint 3c) or an `eq-ref` /
//!   `eq-sym` / `eq-trans` consequence of committed triples, so nothing is
//!   assumed.
//!
//! # What is NOT claimed (read this before quoting the above)
//! * **COMPLETENESS is NOT claimed, in EITHER direction.** The converse of the
//!   THEOREM is false by construction: a prover may simply OMIT entries or
//!   edges, and `canon` is then a strict REFINEMENT of `~`. Under-merging costs
//!   only provability, never soundness -- so a FAILURE to prove a row entailed
//!   proves NOTHING about the row (the same existence-only posture as
//!   `path.nr`). Nor is fixpoint saturation addressed; that is `sq-rsd3v.7`.
//! * **Blank nodes.** Term encodings for blank nodes are SALT-dependent, so a
//!   `sameAs` edge touching a bnode is per-graph and the disclosed `row` of
//!   `sameas_entails_row` would carry a salted encoding. The estate's existing
//!   cross-graph non-bnode (Q6) obligation is NOT discharged here; it stays a
//!   verifier-side obligation, unchanged by this module.
//! * **`sameas_enc` is a PUBLIC constant, not a trusted schema edge.** The
//!   verifier recomputes it from the fixed `owl:sameAs` IRI exactly as
//!   `derivation.nr` recomputes the five RDFS vocabulary encodings. The
//!   equality FACTS are still grounded against `commitments[g]`.
//!
//! # Public vs private (the privacy boundary)
//! * PUBLIC:  `commitments`, `sameas_enc`, `row` (the disclosed derived row).
//!            As in `scan.nr`, a bin-package `main` wrapper would additionally
//!            carry the verifier nonce `challenge` (unconstrained here,
//!            byte-bound verifier-side).
//! * PRIVATE: `enc`/`counts` (the graph), the whole `(mem, rep, parent)` canon
//!            table, and `src_graph`/`src_slot` (WHICH committed triple was
//!            rewritten). So neither the base fact nor the equality chain that
//!            justified the rewrite is disclosed.
//!
//! # Cost (the "cost cliff" -- NO number is claimed here)
//! The dominant terms are `U*K*N` (constraint 3c: each table edge is looked up
//! against the whole in-circuit graph, the `scan.nr` whole-graph membership
//! discipline -- `C(G)` is a flat Poseidon2 sponge with no per-leaf
//! authentication paths, so there is no Merkle shortcut until the deferred
//! `commit.rs` "shape-2" per-graph tree lands), `U*U` (constraint 4), and
//! `K*N + U` for the row anchor. Naming the committed base triple by witnessed
//! `(src_graph, src_slot)` -- rather than canonicalising every slot of every
//! graph -- is what keeps the row anchor at `K*N + U` instead of `K*N*U`.
//! Repo policy FORBIDS an unmeasured cost figure: `bb gates` over a compiled
//! monomorphisation MUST be run before ANY cost claim is made, and it has NOT
//! been run for this module.
//!
//! # HONEST STATUS (sq-qhy4)
//! Research-grade, NOT externally audited; the composition seam it would plug
//! into is the maintainer's own not-yet-sound position. This is the in-circuit
//! RELATION only -- the off-circuit witness generator lives host-side in
//! `crates/sparq-zk-compose/src/sameas.rs` (which mirrors these constraints
//! 1:1), and a compiled `sameas_k{K}_n{N}_u{U}` bin package, its `bb gates`
//! measurement, and the `verify_manifest` dispatch that would bind such a proof
//! are follow-ups. Nothing here makes the composition verifier sound.

use crate::hashes::{commit_fold, h3};

/// Term-encoding-triple equality (an encoding equality IS a term equality over
/// the salt-independent encoding -- the same primitive `derivation.nr` uses).
fn triple_eq(a: [Field; 3], b: [Field; 3]) -> bool {
    (a[0] == b[0]) & (a[1] == b[1]) & (a[2] == b[2])
}

/// `row` occurs in some ACTIVE slot of some committed graph -- the `scan.nr` /
/// `join.nr` / `derivation.nr` whole-graph-in-circuit membership discipline
/// (membership is equality against witnessed slots; no Merkle machinery).
fn row_in_union<let K: u32, let N: u32>(
    enc: [[[Field; 3]; N]; K],
    counts: [u32; K],
    row: [Field; 3],
) -> bool {
    let mut present = false;
    for g in 0..K {
        for i in 0..N {
            present |= (i < counts[g]) & triple_eq(enc[g][i], row);
        }
    }
    present
}

/// The canonical representative of `term` under the witnessed canon table:
/// `rep[u]` for the unique ACTIVE entry with `mem[u] == term`, else `term`
/// itself (a term with no entry is its own class -- OWL-RL `eq-ref`).
///
/// This is a pure READ of the table; it is sound ONLY in combination with
/// [`canon_table_check`], which is what establishes `canon_of(t) ~ t` (L2) and
/// the uniqueness that makes the "unique entry" above well defined (L3). A
/// caller that reads the table without checking it proves nothing.
pub fn canon_of<let U: u32>(
    mem: [Field; U],
    rep: [Field; U],
    entry_count: u32,
    term: Field,
) -> Field {
    let mut out = term;
    for u in 0..U {
        let hit = (u < entry_count) & (mem[u] == term);
        out = if hit { rep[u] } else { out };
    }
    out
}

/// Check the witnessed union-find canon table against the committed graphs.
///
/// The table is a spanning FOREST of the committed `owl:sameAs` edge graph:
/// entry `u` holds a member term `mem[u]`, its class representative `rep[u]`,
/// and `parent[u]` -- the index of the entry it was merged INTO, or `u` itself
/// for a class root. Entries at or beyond `entry_count` are inactive padding
/// and are unconstrained (they are also invisible to [`canon_of`]).
///
/// Constraints (the numbering the module-doc lemmas cite):
/// 3a. `parent[u] <= u`, so a non-root's parent is STRICTLY earlier -- the
///     forest is well-founded and no cycle can fabricate a class.
/// 3b. a root has `rep[u] == mem[u]`; a non-root has `rep[u] == rep[parent[u]]`
///     -- the representative is constant along every tree edge.
/// 3c. every tree edge is JUSTIFIED: `(mem[u], sameAs, mem[parent[u]])` or
///     `(mem[parent[u]], sameAs, mem[u])` occurs in an active slot of some
///     committed graph. This is the anchor -- an unjustified merge (the
///     "claim `alice sameAs bob` and inherit bob's facts" forgery) has no
///     satisfying witness.
/// 4.  members are DISTINCT across active entries, so `canon_of` is a function
///     (needed for idempotence, L3).
///
/// The caller is responsible for having bound `enc`/`counts` to the PUBLIC
/// `commitments` (step 1 of [`sameas_entails_row`]); checking a table against
/// an unbound graph proves nothing.
pub fn canon_table_check<let K: u32, let N: u32, let U: u32>(
    sameas_enc: Field,
    counts: [u32; K],
    enc: [[[Field; 3]; N]; K],
    entry_count: u32,
    mem: [Field; U],
    rep: [Field; U],
    parent: [u32; U],
) {
    assert(entry_count <= U, "entry_count exceeds circuit bucket");

    // 4. Distinct members across active entries (`j < u` plus `u < entry_count`
    //    makes both active), so `canon_of` is single-valued. Checked FIRST
    //    because well-definedness is the precondition the lemmas assume; the
    //    number matches the module-doc citation, not the physical order.
    for u in 0..U {
        for j in 0..U {
            let both = (u < entry_count) & (j < u);
            assert(!both | (mem[u] != mem[j]), "duplicate member in canon table");
        }
    }

    for u in 0..U {
        let active = u < entry_count;
        let is_root = parent[u] == u;

        // 3a. No forward reference: a non-root points STRICTLY backwards.
        assert(!active | (parent[u] <= u), "canon parent is not an earlier entry");

        // Select the parent entry's member + representative. `p < u` and
        // `u < entry_count` together make the parent active, so no separate
        // activity check is needed. For a root nothing is selected and the
        // accumulators stay 0 -- harmless, since every use below is masked by
        // `!is_root`.
        let mut p_mem: Field = 0;
        let mut p_rep: Field = 0;
        for j in 0..U {
            let sel = (j < u) & (parent[u] == j);
            p_mem = if sel { mem[j] } else { p_mem };
            p_rep = if sel { rep[j] } else { p_rep };
        }

        // 3b. The representative is constant along the tree edge; a root is its
        //     own representative.
        assert(
            !active | !is_root | (rep[u] == mem[u]),
            "canon root is not its own representative",
        );
        assert(
            !active | is_root | (rep[u] == p_rep),
            "canon entry disagrees with its parent's representative",
        );

        // 3c. The merge is JUSTIFIED by a committed owl:sameAs triple, in
        //     EITHER orientation (owl:sameAs is symmetric -- OWL-RL eq-sym).
        let fwd = row_in_union(enc, counts, [mem[u], sameas_enc, p_mem]);
        let bwd = row_in_union(enc, counts, [p_mem, sameas_enc, mem[u]]);
        assert(
            !active | is_root | fwd | bwd,
            "canon merge not justified by a committed owl:sameAs triple",
        );
    }
}

/// The `owl:sameAs` equality-reasoning relation: prove the PUBLIC disclosed
/// `row` is entailed by the committed graphs under OWL-RL `eq-*`, WITHOUT
/// disclosing the base triple it was rewritten from or the equality chain that
/// justified the rewrite.
///
/// Constraint walk (numbered like `scan.nr` / `derivation.nr`):
/// 1. **Commitment recompute** -- every witnessed slot re-hashes (`h3` leaf,
///    `commit_fold` over the first `counts[g]`) to the PUBLIC `commitments[g]`,
///    so both the `sameAs` edges and the base triple below are membership in
///    the SAME `C(G)` the issuer signed, not a free-floating set.
/// 2. **Canon table** -- [`canon_table_check`] (constraints 3a-3c, 4).
/// 3. **Representative-membership** -- the prover names the base triple by
///    witnessed `(src_graph, src_slot)`; it must be an ACTIVE slot, and its
///    three canonical representatives must equal the disclosed row's. By the
///    module-doc COROLLARY the row is then entailed.
///
/// `src_graph`/`src_slot` are PRIVATE: which committed triple was rewritten is
/// not disclosed. NO cost claim -- see the module-doc cost note (`bb gates` has
/// not been run). NOT externally audited (sq-qhy4).
pub fn sameas_entails_row<let K: u32, let N: u32, let U: u32>(
    commitments: [Field; K],
    sameas_enc: Field,
    row: [Field; 3],
    counts: [u32; K],
    enc: [[[Field; 3]; N]; K],
    entry_count: u32,
    mem: [Field; U],
    rep: [Field; U],
    parent: [u32; U],
    src_graph: u32,
    src_slot: u32,
) {
    // 1. Commitment recompute (identical to scan.nr / derivation.nr step 1).
    for g in 0..K {
        assert(counts[g] <= N, "graph size exceeds circuit bucket");
        let mut leaves: [Field; N] = [0; N];
        for i in 0..N {
            leaves[i] = h3(enc[g][i][0], enc[g][i][1], enc[g][i][2]);
        }
        assert_eq(commit_fold(leaves, counts[g]), commitments[g], "graph commitment mismatch");
    }

    // 2. The canon table is a justified spanning forest of the committed
    //    owl:sameAs edges.
    canon_table_check(sameas_enc, counts, enc, entry_count, mem, rep, parent);

    // 3. Representative-membership. Select the witnessed base triple; the
    //    `i < counts[g]` mask means a padding slot can never be selected, so a
    //    fabricated base fact smuggled into padding has no witness.
    let mut base: [Field; 3] = [0; 3];
    let mut found = false;
    for g in 0..K {
        for i in 0..N {
            let sel = (g == src_graph) & (i == src_slot) & (i < counts[g]);
            base[0] = if sel { enc[g][i][0] } else { base[0] };
            base[1] = if sel { enc[g][i][1] } else { base[1] };
            base[2] = if sel { enc[g][i][2] } else { base[2] };
            found |= sel;
        }
    }
    assert(found, "witnessed base triple is not an active committed slot");

    // eq-rep-s / eq-rep-p / eq-rep-o, one per slot, via the canonical form.
    for s in 0..3 {
        assert(
            canon_of(mem, rep, entry_count, base[s])
                == canon_of(mem, rep, entry_count, row[s]),
            "disclosed row is not the committed triple up to owl:sameAs",
        );
    }
}
